  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Extreme/Excessive heat will remain a concern into\nearly next week.\n\nUPDATE: As of 11 AM, the heat index at KCHS was 106 and 106-108\nacross eastern portions of Berkeley County per mesonet and RAWS\nobservations. Indices were already 105-106 within the RAWS \nnetwork across the Fort Stewart region. The Heat Advisory for \ninland portions of Berkeley, Jasper, Bryan and Liberty Counties \nhas been upgraded to an Extreme Heat Warning given these trends.\n\nToday into Next Week: A large-scale ridge across much of the South \nCentral United States will expand eastward today through late week, \nbecoming centered across the Deep South and Southeast United States \nand favoring a warm west/northwesterly aloft. Ample sunshine will \npromote strong sfc heating during the next couple days, with 1000-\n850 mb thickness levels supportive of afternoon highs in the mid-\nupper 90s, with some locations touching 100 degrees away from the \ncoast through much of the weekend. In addition to hot temperatures, \nconditions will be quite humid with sfc dewpts in the mid-upper 70s \neach day. Although some mixing out of sfc dewpts is anticipated \ninland with the downslope wind setup locally, a sea breeze will \neventually shift higher moisture content onshore each day for at \nleast a few hours. This will likely result in the higher \nheat/moisture combination along coastal zones (along/east of I-95) \nfor the next couple days, with heat index values up to the 110-115 \nrange this afternoon and again Friday afternoon. Further inland, \nheat index values should top out in the 105-110 degree range. Given \nthe setup and expected heat index values, an Extreme Heat Warning \nremains in effect for coastal counties from 11 AM to 8 PM today \nwhile remaining areas are included in a Heat Advisory for the same \ntime period. The slightly more favorable position of the synoptic \nridge supports slightly higher heat index values along the coastal \ncorridor Friday. Therefore, an Extreme Heat Watch has been issued \nfor all Southeast SC/GA coastal zones from 11 AM to 8 PM Friday \nafternoon. Another Heat Advisory could be needed for inland areas on \nFriday as well.  \n\nHot/humid conditions likely remain across the Southeast United \nStates late weekend into early next week with the large-scale ridge \ncentered over or nearby, although a front could lead to greater \nshower/thunderstorm coverage that limits maximum heat conditions. \nAdditional heat headlines could be needed through early next\nweek. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: There is a risk of strong thunderstorms today.\n\nStrong ridging aloft will persist over the southeastern states today \nwhile a shortwave ripples across the northern periphery of the \nridge. CAMs have struggled the last few days to get a handle on \nconvection given the northwesterly flow at the surface, but the \nshortwave could be enough forcing to trigger thunderstorms across \nthe SC Midlands. The latest runs of the HRRR and the 00Z HREF depict \nsome convection firing in the later afternoon hours and tracking \ninto the SC Lowcountry and Southeastern GA into the evening. \nAdditionally, isolated convection is possible with the afternoon sea \nbreeze. Unlike on Wednesday, the sea breeze shouldn't be pinned at \nthe coast and looks to be progressive this afternoon. The forecast \nfeatures slight chance thunderstorms this afternoon. Any storms that \nare able to form or track into the forecast area have the chance to \nbecome strong. The environment this afternoon is forecast to have\nDCAPE values upwards of 1000 J/kg. The main threat this afternoon\nwould be damaging wind gusts with any of the stronger thunderstorms.",
